#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/id #uuid "3819a294-7975-5c5c-b3d8-100032d5cd33" :question/math-academy-id "e-9040" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t),$ $x_2(t) = y'(t)$ and $x_3(t) = y''(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2,$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\,y'' - t^3 y' + 4t^2 y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\, x_3 - t^3 x_2 + 4t^2 x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=4{t}^{2}\\,{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+3\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = 4t^2 x_1 - t^3 x_2 + 3\\sqrt{t} \\,x_3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166633 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/id #uuid "529c6004-c3f2-5e7a-8a04-8fff4f886ab8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9041" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''  + y' - 6y= 0 , \\quad y(0)= 1, \\quad y'(0)= 4\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t),$ $x_2(t) = y'(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = - y' + 6y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = - x_2 + 6x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=1, q=4, f(t) = 6x_1 - x_2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(1-4)(6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3(6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "e81f0b0b-985f-5e01-b821-fde2aa8bc7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9138" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation $y'' + 5y' - 2y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/59e386be8b30fca475a2e0d164687a6561b9ab368b9879fc301d328896d184fd.png)\nFind the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''= -5y' + 2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = -5x_2 + 2x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/f35de5e6f066144c4130e38304abbff552730dc1fe97828572a66e68970c5d52.png)\n\nHence, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/314f9dcf277b61dac9985c448aed98113516592e3822cc47a8566d28a480651b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79bb2fe-b1ab-5545-b51f-6dfa50fd6b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(2x_{1} - 3x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163389} {:answer/id #uuid "b85b6a7c-695a-593a-8228-839bd1d88776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x_{1} + 3x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163390} {:answer/id #uuid "2aec8325-b896-5946-a1a3-b5fef93e5f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2x_{1} - 3x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163391} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6ece57-ea87-58f1-9e69-1e24a88cb866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2x_{1} - 3x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163392} {:answer/id #uuid "7df1dd86-c1f3-584e-a95e-239282bc37c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x_{1} - 3x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7df1dd86-c1f3-584e-a95e-239282bc37c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x_{1} - 3x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163393} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a03a451-d462-541c-bf05-614cba58c578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80a38704-3722-5fd2-9d30-5b217f5c3710" :question/math-academy-id "q-107906" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}-2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=3,f(t)=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-3)f(t) \\\\  & =-f(t) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e0ad053-cf7a-570c-9197-f6484c8e1947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366310} {:answer/id #uuid "85209c38-b0dd-5e37-be15-626b1d4fab54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10{x}_{1}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366311} {:answer/id #uuid "bd27a512-79b2-5a25-b37f-3b18aa046949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366312} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe48242-a69c-535f-8804-bf70c7ecfa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366313} {:answer/id #uuid "1f66bee2-8cc5-554d-bb48-2a7ea4665e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fe48242-a69c-535f-8804-bf70c7ecfa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366313} :answer-field/id #uuid "56d954e1-cf8b-53a7-894c-8d1f243cfe90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95129f08-fcca-5b68-9041-afb0f8ffaa61" :question/math-academy-id "q-109582" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}-10y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}+10y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}+10{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1f2bda3-822a-5085-bcb1-fe989d1af220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{t}{x}_{1}+4t{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366317} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b075cf-3d29-5cae-968b-6a8c8c052c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t{x}_{1}-4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366318} {:answer/id #uuid "912e92de-64d4-5de5-8201-cd0aee021cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{t}{x}_{1}+4t{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366319} {:answer/id #uuid "11c50a90-5386-5dd2-999f-495fe88a92b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-4t{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366320} {:answer/id #uuid "021b3d81-3569-588e-a3f0-e573183da9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021b3d81-3569-588e-a3f0-e573183da9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366321} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c9c29c-cee1-5d71-8987-34ec050275c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1efb3be1-4590-5921-8a36-f0d39f3dba85" :question/math-academy-id "q-109583" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+4ty=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}-4ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}-4t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-4t{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d44693c0-8182-57b4-9e59-c3622ab5da71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366324} {:answer/id #uuid "c56fcd49-7f91-5e0f-a992-a1b2dca68222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366325} {:answer/id #uuid "85737b0d-4d60-5db4-8f82-d1cee0cf084a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366326} {:answer/id #uuid "bd576833-92a7-5105-bd30-98002bec0059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366327} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb81b7a-5276-5f4f-b9cf-a2e70f17c14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}-2\\,{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56fcd49-7f91-5e0f-a992-a1b2dca68222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366325} :answer-field/id #uuid "934b1a8e-3165-5257-b908-7744e983e91d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33461068-ee53-56f4-bf58-a85f4de3c6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109623" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{″}-{t}^{3}{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{″}+{t}^{3}{y}^{′}-2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-2{x}_{1}+{t}^{3}{x}_{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d534b33-f0f7-5354-8174-ebb091036fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{t}^{3/2}{x}_{1}+3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366331} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa5db83-b7a2-5ee8-a9ad-9e67339330ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366332} {:answer/id #uuid "695f46f4-2e2c-5ba7-8644-d5380cfcf6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366333} {:answer/id #uuid "396a3dee-7301-57f7-8964-225828703484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{1}-7{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366334} {:answer/id #uuid "9d45fcbc-d98a-5a35-8133-4a09692e98fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{x}_{2}+3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa5db83-b7a2-5ee8-a9ad-9e67339330ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366332} :answer-field/id #uuid "383c2f86-9bc0-5a71-94a8-0e30c204d929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a5c4536-304f-519c-bfd6-2a671aa1ec81" :question/math-academy-id "q-109628" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+3{t}^{1/2}{y}^{″}-7y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{t}^{1/2}{y}^{″}+7y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}+7{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=7{x}_{1}-3{t}^{1/2}{x}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bdf1b36-96bb-51b4-9e8b-e0129eb0162e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}+t{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366338} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb511c8-2490-553b-968e-e508805d810b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}-t{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366339} {:answer/id #uuid "09e254ea-3081-5109-bf47-53e305d2f105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366340} {:answer/id #uuid "7b52d687-6d33-5901-b0c9-0cb6ce148a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366341} {:answer/id #uuid "cb416fa1-288f-5267-b442-bf47af884c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{x}_{1}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b52d687-6d33-5901-b0c9-0cb6ce148a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366341} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0fe0af-3fcb-59b1-b6c8-2707bd0394ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75dfee07-3584-54db-951a-175908c71eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-109630" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+{e}^{t}{y}^{″}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{e}^{t}{y}^{″}+{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}+{e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)={e}^{\\sqrt{t}}{x}_{1}-{e}^{t}{x}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97fabf27-aab3-5983-bb6b-3f3a75309842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(4{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366345} {:answer/id #uuid "27181915-31b3-5850-95e5-7f1611824014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(4{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366346} {:answer/id #uuid "91cd9371-5de8-5c5a-8ada-db21a7d50993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366347} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f49976-b5e6-5ceb-b6a6-d1a50892cc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366348} {:answer/id #uuid "67842a88-c857-5310-b921-0889037ea3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6f49976-b5e6-5ceb-b6a6-d1a50892cc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366348} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f70100-3dee-5d38-8467-138a8312efae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7a70976-fd43-5b84-a619-3ddab5c39c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109638" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-ty=0,\\,y(2)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & ={x}_{1}(2)=-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(2) & ={x}_{2}(2)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-4{y}^{′}+ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-4{x}_{2}+t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-2,q=-3,f(t)=t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(-2-(-3))(t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =(1)(t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =t{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d5b9e2b-6059-5789-bb8f-04297f72632e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366352} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf758bb-930c-5989-a8dd-fac3f629bb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366353} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd7130b-2cff-501f-acd4-54f12cd300fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366354} {:answer/id #uuid "25e6dad9-df83-5646-b630-1545316ac404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(-12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366355} {:answer/id #uuid "58841aa9-ecd9-58fe-b8f3-1ef30f299dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(-3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+12{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5b9e2b-6059-5789-bb8f-04297f72632e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366352} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2f899e-a53c-52a5-af8f-ea505b5e2066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "250135ef-e54c-5b53-9ec4-19d900601af6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109641" :question/problem "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+3{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}-12y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q+r)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(0) & ={x}_{3}(0)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+12y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=1,r=3,f(t)=12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q+r)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-1+3)(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =4(12{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c9ea6cc-893e-5545-a4f4-83353395b4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366359} {:answer/id #uuid "f989cdf3-7473-5902-bf7e-53360b092941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366360} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6ef9a7-0537-536c-86a8-a403320e72a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366361} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7714ec-4823-564d-8635-fde5f41e3cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366362} {:answer/id #uuid "064eef02-ab1f-5141-98f7-d23c632da105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9ea6cc-893e-5545-a4f4-83353395b4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366359} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01c3cb2-69d4-5a1d-9b22-269fa2b92125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c382da1a-1bd0-5d53-b506-6fe5031cfb29" :question/math-academy-id "q-109642" :question/problem "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+3{y}^{′}=0,\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1,\\,{y}^{″}(2)=2\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(2)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate $(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & ={x}_{1}(2)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(2) & ={x}_{2}(2)=1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(2) & ={x}_{3}(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(2)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(2)=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-3{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{3}(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-1,q=1,r=2,f(t)=-3{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(-1+1-2)(-3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =(-2)(-3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =6{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3beeede1-a4ea-561f-93c6-f9f368428c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(6{x}_{2}-3\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366366} {:answer/id #uuid "d07bfd76-df1b-5d78-abe7-4c787c512aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366367} {:answer/id #uuid "d57f8041-d4a1-545e-a8b8-23250fe627b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366368} {:answer/id #uuid "4f29e7fd-7443-53e8-823a-6d9c057dc2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8(6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366369} {:answer/id #uuid "4b372f60-bd6c-5e27-9d7e-7a01f68b742b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d57f8041-d4a1-545e-a8b8-23250fe627b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366368} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a9d72e-88bb-5e94-9846-81022cf4dc22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53f5f48b-e375-5e2f-99ed-48004360183f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109643" :question/problem "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-6\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(1)=-3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(1)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{″}(1) & ={x}_{3}(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=6\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}-12{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-2,q=2,r=-3,f(t)=-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(-2+2-(-3))(-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-2+2+3)(-12{x}_{2}+6\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(-6)(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-18(2{x}_{2}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20740ef5-cb16-5776-8c6b-0fe3ef7e8a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366373} {:answer/id #uuid "786bd0e1-3964-5b58-b0f2-c86469191f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366374} {:answer/id #uuid "a18b2a01-5e4c-58e2-9412-5463c0dbbcad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366375} {:answer/id #uuid "379e21da-82bd-5cbf-b974-6f7040f62156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366376} {:answer/id #uuid "738e0830-d962-576d-834e-20d66227135a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a18b2a01-5e4c-58e2-9412-5463c0dbbcad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366375} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d81be2-b251-5020-9de9-afbdb44ce8f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7713dc2-84ca-5bb4-a57a-9ba6579282a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109646" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+3y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{y}^{′}-3y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28465812-4bdb-56d6-b1a8-f14ed5d801a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366380} {:answer/id #uuid "ae43508a-6545-5ea9-ab3f-36babccbdb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366381} {:answer/id #uuid "43e01697-c1d7-500d-8b9c-7db3788b5dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{3} & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366382} {:answer/id #uuid "886cb56a-fb01-5225-acd8-211db5b29c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{3} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366383} {:answer/id #uuid "278b9b9a-dddd-5b5e-aa90-4a09860dbb68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae43508a-6545-5ea9-ab3f-36babccbdb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366381} :answer-field/id #uuid "68c0d915-ab28-5b50-9db8-d473be3d03d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0e82d28-4193-5245-8695-f97b44951b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-109692" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{1}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce8c5384-ddee-5573-9d7e-067e5948d126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366387} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f1240-9dfe-5491-bb44-93852b6b634f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366388} {:answer/id #uuid "4597d81d-9c57-5508-820e-c001533c89a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3{t}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366389} {:answer/id #uuid "027cf64a-6ec3-580b-a9c6-6b9f3a0d6e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366390} {:answer/id #uuid "fd789455-28ee-5696-b105-c4946a505917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce8c5384-ddee-5573-9d7e-067e5948d126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366387} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3bdcc9-f3c5-5474-958a-5362aa3718eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0737f5ff-3d66-5f51-b7b8-b8bf8fe97f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-109695" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+5{y}^{″}-3{t}^{2}{y}^{′}=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & a & c\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-5{y}^{″}+3{t}^{2}{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-5{x}_{3}+3{t}^{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=3{t}^{2}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3{t}^{2} & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=3{t}^{2},b=1,c=-5,$ and $a+b+c=3{t}^{2}-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df3f7cb-645a-57bf-ab31-18fb013189b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366394} {:answer/id #uuid "caf0f5a2-c0e1-53ef-8f3f-63dd76973658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366395} {:answer/id #uuid "8691544e-d94b-55df-9c58-7b5a0ee99681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366396} {:answer/id #uuid "86287066-75f9-5b18-a5af-c42b6647e606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366397} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dd2f67-b61f-5d54-a262-2e1a780f0d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86287066-75f9-5b18-a5af-c42b6647e606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366397} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce21225-5b3e-5167-97af-ca985147f3ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854fff95-597d-57db-aef7-0e595418f814" :question/math-academy-id "q-109696" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-3{y}^{′}+4y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ a & c & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=3{y}^{′}-4y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=-4,b=1,c=3,$ and $a+b+c=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33931923-d925-577d-8770-bef2a1650629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366401} {:answer/id #uuid "17049e80-8f02-5d15-8fc8-7277e3f1915b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366402} {:answer/id #uuid "444d6424-0929-520f-899b-be78dcc9ae4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366403} {:answer/id #uuid "7af81163-828b-573b-9354-788acd2fdccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366404} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbb47cd-2316-5f76-a401-54289ce15a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444d6424-0929-520f-899b-be78dcc9ae4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366403} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c0b23f-a466-51e2-ab19-c42f45659baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df09efa8-0851-5057-97e5-5c263f3619c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109698" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}+13{y}^{′}=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & b & c\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=2{y}^{″}-13{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=2{x}_{3}-13{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-13{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=1,b=-13,c=2,$ and $a+b+c=-10.$"}]}
